{"ts": 0.058958, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\u001b[90m──\u001b[0m \u001b[90mModule · shells\u001b[0m \u001b[90m───────────────────────────────────────────────────\u001b[0m \u001b[93m1 of 3\u001b[0m \u001b[90m──\u001b[0m\r\n", "len": 238}
{"ts": 0.059008, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\r\n", "len": 2}
{"ts": 0.061911, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\u001b[1;97mshells 1 — the shell is a program\u001b[0m\r\n\r\nThe shell reads the line you type, finds the program you named, runs\r\nit, and shows what it printed. The shell is itself a program.\r\n\r\n  \u001b[92mecho $BASH_VERSION\u001b[0m   \u001b[90m# the version of the shell running now\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[92mps\u001b[0m                    \u001b[90m# one line is this shell, one is ps itself\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n", "len": 354}
{"ts": 0.061983, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\u001b[92m$BASH_VERSION\u001b[0m is a \u001b[36mvariable\u001b[0m: a name that holds a value. The\r\nshell swaps \u001b[92m$name\u001b[0m for its value before it runs the line.\r\n\r\nNotice that \u001b[92mps\u001b[0m lists the shell next to the command you just ran.\r\n\r\n\u001b[93m▶ Run both commands, then type \u001b[92mnext\u001b[93m.\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n", "len": 286}
{"ts": 0.06527, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\u001b[1;90mnext\u001b[0m \u001b[90m(go on)\u001b[0m   \u001b[1;90mshow\u001b[0m \u001b[90m(this screen again)\u001b[0m   \u001b[1;90mprev\u001b[0m \u001b[90m(back)\u001b[0m   \u001b[1;90mquit\u001b[0m \u001b[90m(save and leave)\u001b[0m\r\n", "len": 159}
{"ts": 0.065552, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\u001b[90m────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n", "len": 253}
