{"ts": 0.116361, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\u001b[1;97mshells 3 — your login shell and this shell\u001b[0m\r\n\r\nYour account has a \u001b[36mlogin shell\u001b[0m: the one a new terminal window\r\nstarts. It can differ from the shell running now.\r\n\r\n", "len": 184}
{"ts": 0.116434, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "  \u001b[92mecho $SHELL\u001b[0m          \u001b[90m# your login shell\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[92mecho $BASH_VERSION\u001b[0m   \u001b[90m# the shell running now\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[92m$SHELL\u001b[0m comes from your account. It does not change when you start\r\nanother shell from inside it.\r\n\r\nThis tutor runs its lessons in Bash whatever your login shell is, so\r\nevery learner sees the same thing. The programs you practise here\r\nbehave the same under \u001b[92mzsh\u001b[0m.\r\n\r\n\u001b[93m▶ Compare the two lines, then type \u001b[92mnext\u001b[93m to take the gate.\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n", "len": 496}
